        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-693",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In lib/sanitize.js:14, the guard `if (hasOwn(options,'allowedTags') && Array.isArray(options.allowedTags) && options.allowedTags.length > 0)` silently DISCARDS the strictest possible caller configuration, `allowedTags: []` (\"allow no HTML tags at all\"). Because the `.length > 0` clause is false for an empty array, line 15 `sanitizerOptions.allowedTags = options.allowedTags` never executes, so `sanitizerOptions` is handed to sanitize-html with no `allowedTags` key and the library reverts to its permissive built-in default tag allowlist. The protection the integrator explicitly requested is reverted to a weaker default with no error or warning. Blast radius is total: `initializeOptions(options)` at line 67 is the highest-centrality call in the whole graph (0.004966) and its result FLOWS_TO every sanitizeHtml sink (lines 37, 42, 57), so the downgrade applies to every sanitized value from req.body/params/headers/query.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: lib/sanitize.js:14 does carry the `&& options.allowedTags.length > 0` clause, so the strictest caller config `allowedTags: []` makes the condition false, line 15 never executes, and `sanitizerOptions` reaches sanitize-html with no `allowedTags` key \u2014 where it is merged against sanitize-html's `defaults` and the built-in allowlist (p, div, a, h1-h6, table, span, b, i, ...) applies instead of the requested zero tags, silently and without warning. The clause is not load-bearing: test/test.js:1300-1316 (`Object.prototype.allowedTags = ['script']`) shows the prototype-pollution defense is supplied entirely by `hasOwn`/Reflect.ownKeys (lines 7-10), so `.length > 0` adds no hardening and only discards legitimate empty-array config. The same fail-open exists at line 18 for `allowedAttributes: {}`. Reach is confirmed in source, not just the graph: index.js:5-20 middleware calls sanitize() on req.body/params/headers (line 9) and req.query (line 12) -> prepareSanitize -> initializeOptions(options) at lib/sanitize.js:67 -> sanitizeHtml sinks at 37/42/57. I correct the analyst on two points. (1) Blast radius is NOT total: the downgrade only fires for integrators who pass `allowedTags: []` or `allowedAttributes: {}`; the default no-option path is unaffected. (2) CWE-79 is too strong \u2014 sanitize-html's defaults exclude script/iframe/img/svg/style/object and all on* attributes, and allowedSchemes blocks javascript:, so the achievable impact is HTML/markup injection, attacker-controlled hyperlinks, UI-redress/phishing and content spoofing into output the integrator believed was tag-free, not script execution. That makes this a protection-mechanism failure (CWE-693) at MEDIUM severity rather than critical XSS. Exploit-reference check: sanitize-html is pinned at ~2.13.0 (package.json:34, matched by :Dependency), but exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no module/CVE version range could be compared; this is in any case a defect in this repo's own wrapper logic, not in the pinned dependency."
          },
