        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "OBJECT KEYS ARE NEVER SANITIZED \u2014 only values are. In the object branch of `sanitize` (lib/sanitize.js:51-59) the walk is `Object.keys(data).forEach((key) => { ... const item = data[key]; if (typeof item === 'string') { data[key] = sanitizeHtml(item, options.sanitizerOptions); } else if (...) { data[key] = sanitize(options, item, currentDepth + 1); } })`. The loop variable `key` is read (to index `data`) and re-used verbatim as the destination property name, but is never itself passed through `sanitizeHtml` and the property is never renamed. An attacker POSTing `{\"<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\": \"v\"}` as JSON body, or sending `?<svg onload=alert(1)>=v` as a query string, gets the malicious markup preserved intact on the object the middleware hands downstream as \"sanitized\". Any consumer that reflects field NAMES into HTML (e.g. rendering `Object.keys(req.body)` in a validation-error message, a form-rebuild template, or an admin dump of submitted fields) is XSS-able straight through the sanitizer. The missing control is applying sanitizeHtml to `key` and reassigning under the cleaned name.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed: express-xss-sanitizer's object walk sanitizes only property VALUES, never property NAMES. In lib/sanitize.js:50-61 the loop variable `key` is used solely to test `options.allowedKeys.includes(key)` (:52), read `data[key]` (:55), and write back to `data[key]` (:57, :59) \u2014 it is never passed through sanitizeHtml and the property is never renamed. A graph sweep shows this is systematic, not a missed branch: there are exactly 3 sanitizeHtml call sites in the whole scan (lib/sanitize.js:37, :42, :57) and every argument is a value (`data`/`item`), so no key-sanitization path exists anywhere in the package. The path is genuinely reachable, so the lead does not fail the exploitability test: the object-branch forEach at :51 is reachable_from_entry=true (hop 1) and sanitizeHtml at :57 is reachable_from_entry=true (hop 2); index.js:7-18 invokes it per-request for body/params/headers/query. Keys reach the consumer intact because sanitize mutates in place and returns the same object (`return data`, :63), which index.js:9 reassigns to req[k] and :13-18 redefines req.query as. Attacker control of keys is real for JSON bodies ({\"<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\":\"v\"} is valid JSON) and query strings (qs parses ?<svg onload=alert(1)>=v into that literal key); the headers vector is NOT viable since Node lowercases and rejects invalid header-name characters. This is an undisclosed gap in a dedicated security control: package.json:4 and README.md:2 promise unqualified XSS sanitization of req.body/query/headers/params, allowedKeys treats keys only as selectors (README:35-38), and no test asserts key sanitization. Scoped to MEDIUM, not higher, because the XSS sink is out-of-repo: exploitation requires a downstream consumer that reflects field NAMES into HTML (validation-error messages, form-rebuild templates, admin dumps of submitted fields) \u2014 realistic but less common than value reflection, which the library handles correctly. Corrections to the analyst: the forEach closes at line 61 (not 59), and the exploit-reference check yielded no signal in either direction \u2014 mcp__orion__exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\" \u2014 so despite the version being exactly pinned (express-xss-sanitizer 2.0.1, sanitize-html ~2.13.0) I claim neither a weaponized exploit nor patched status, and no part of this verdict rests on that check."
          },
          "locations": [
