        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1188",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "FAIL-OPEN ALLOWLIST: `initializeOptions` only forwards `allowedTags`/`allowedAttributes` to sanitize-html when they are NON-EMPTY, so the two strictest configurations an integrator can express silently become the most permissive. At lib/sanitize.js:14 the guard is `hasOwn(options,'allowedTags') && Array.isArray(options.allowedTags) && options.allowedTags.length > 0`, and at :18 it is `hasOwn(options,'allowedAttributes') && Object.keys(options.allowedAttributes).length > 0`. An app that passes `allowedTags: []` (meaning \"strip ALL HTML\") or `allowedAttributes: {}` (meaning \"allow NO attributes\") fails both length checks, so `sanitizerOptions` is left WITHOUT those keys and is handed to `sanitizeHtml(data, options.sanitizerOptions)` at lib/sanitize.js:37/42/57 \u2014 where sanitize-html then applies its own built-in DEFAULT allowlist (which permits `<a href>`, `<img src>`, `<b>`, `<i>`, etc.) instead of stripping everything. The missing control is a distinction between \"option absent\" and \"option present but empty\"; the empty case must produce `allowedTags: []` explicitly rather than be dropped. Verifier should confirm by instantiating `xss({allowedTags: []})` and POSTing `<a href=x>hi</a>` \u2014 the anchor is expected to survive.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: lib/sanitize.js:14 guards the allowedTags forward with `options.allowedTags.length > 0` and :18 guards allowedAttributes with `Object.keys(...).length > 0`, so the two strictest configurations an integrator can express (`allowedTags: []` = \"strip all HTML\", `allowedAttributes: {}` = \"allow no attributes\") fail the length test and are silently DROPPED, leaving `sanitizerOptions` as the bare `{}` created at :13. That `{}` is passed straight to sanitizeHtml at :37/:42/:57, where sanitize-html merges it over its own permissive default allowlist. I did not take the dependency's behavior on faith: the repo's OWN test suite proves it, because `xss({maxDepth:100})` produces the identical empty `sanitizerOptions`, and test/test.js:264-311 asserts that path yields `<a href=\"/\" onclick=\"return 0;\">Link</a>` -> `<a href=\"/\">Link</a>` and `<p onclick=\"return;\">Test</p>` -> `<p>Test</p>`. So an integrator asking for total stripping instead gets the default allowlist (`<a href>`, `<p>`, `<b>`, `<i>`, `<h1>`-`<h6>`, tables) \u2014 a fail-open protection-mechanism failure. Reach is real, not merely graph-asserted: index.js:5-21 applies this to req.body/params/headers/query on every request and the graph shows lines 14/18 and all three sinks reachable_from_entry=true (hop 1-2). README.md:46-53 documents allowedTags without any warning that an empty array is ignored, so the behavior is undocumented, not an intentional contract. TWO ANALYST ERRORS CORRECTED: (1) `<img src>` does NOT survive \u2014 img is absent from sanitize-html 2.x default allowedTags and the repo's own tests (test/test.js:161-170, 196-206) assert `<img src=\"/\"/>` -> `''` under defaults; (2) severity is MEDIUM not HIGH \u2014 the fallback is sanitize-html's hardened default, which still strips `<script>`, all `on*` handlers, and `javascript:` URLs (default allowedSchemes http/https/ftp/mailto/tel), so the impact is HTML/markup and phishing-link injection, not direct script-execution XSS. Caveat on method: no Bash tool and no node_modules in the tree, so I could not execute the live PoC; the confirmation rests on the unambiguous `> 0` guards plus the repo's own passing assertions of the empty-options path. sanitize-html is `~2.13.0` with no lockfile (not exactly pinnable) and exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so there is no exploit-reference grounding either way \u2014 neither matters, since the defect is in this repo's code rather than a dependency CVE."
          },
